WEBVTT

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:09.433
This is RuleShift Live on my Twitch channel,

00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:10.900
streamed from a browser.

00:00:10.917 --> 00:00:12.683
Now, the connected game in close-up.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:18.100
On Twitch, chat usually reacts to the game.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.517
RuleShift Live lets chat change it.

00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.467
Viewers propose a rule, the streamer approves it,

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:26.050
and the rule changes actual game physics.

00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.033
This real Twitch message became a proposal:

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.933
every third coin reverses gravity for four seconds.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.850
The streamer has already approved it,

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.767
and the viewer keeps the credit.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.767
Chat cannot activate a rule by itself.

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:52.383
This proposal was approved before recording.

00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:54.367
I review its exact trigger and effect,

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.000
then start the round.

00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:58.383
One approved rule controls this arena.

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:07.167
On the third coin, acceleration reverses
and the player rises toward the ceiling.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.217
Four seconds later, gravity returns automatically.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.717
Movement and collisions follow the rule.

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:18.983
I keep playing with the same keyboard controls.

00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:21.917
Another third-coin trigger refreshes the effect,

00:01:21.917 --> 00:01:24.733
while the arena and its solid platforms stay playable.

00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:29.117
At the end of the seventy-five-second round,

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:30.817
the server closes the result.

00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:34.900
Effects clear, movement stops,
and the final score is delivered.

00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:39.967
The result is confirmed in PostgreSQL.

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:42.133
I can download a round record with the rule,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.633
its author, the originating message,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.533
and observed gameplay events.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.967
Unsupported requests cannot be approved.

00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:53.967
Chat is never executed as code.

00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:56.783
Supported proposals pass through a bounded queue,

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:59.733
and the streamer keeps control over every activation.

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:04.933
I used Codex to build the Go application boundaries,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.667
Twitch integration, and TypeScript game engine.

00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:12.467
Here, Codex reviews the real rule
and expiry logic and runs the checks.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:15.883
Tests cover race safety, PostgreSQL persistence,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.817
transport failures, and the game behavior.

00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:26.367
The implementation keeps domain rules
independent from Twitch and storage.

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:30.917
RuleShift Live turns audience suggestions
into reversible gameplay changes,

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:35.000
with streamer approval and an
automatic return to normal gravity.
